In this lesson, we will understand the transformer architecture, which is the foundation of modern large language models.

159
00:12:31,456 --> 00:12:40,176
Everything that makes LLMs powerful, such as understanding context, generating text, answering questions, translating languages, and handling long sentences,

160
00:12:40,336 --> 00:12:41,572
comes from this architecture.

161
00:12:42,126 --> 00:12:46,628
We will first look at the paper Attention is All You Need, which introduced Transformers in 2017.

162
00:12:46,736 --> 00:12:51,908
Then we'll understand tokenization and embeddings, which convert raw text into numbers that a model can process.

163
00:12:52,256 --> 00:13:00,976
After that, we will cover self-attention, multi-head attention, positional encoding, the complete transformer block, and three main transformer types that is encoder only,

164
00:13:01,216 --> 00:13:03,268
decoder only, and encoder decoder.

165
00:13:03,630 --> 00:13:08,900
So by the end of this lesson, you will understand why transformers became the backbone of modern AI systems.

166
00:13:09,614 --> 00:13:10,628
So let's get started.

167
00:13:10,896 --> 00:13:12,576
The paper that changed everything.

168
00:13:12,750 --> 00:13:17,456
The transformer architecture was introduced in the 2017 paper Attention is All You Need.

169
00:13:17,696 --> 00:13:23,812
This paper became the foundation of modern AI because almost every major LLM today is based on transformers.

170
00:13:24,782 --> 00:13:30,180
So models like GPT, Claude, Lama, Gemini, and Mistral all use transformer-based architecture.

171
00:13:30,510 --> 00:13:34,976
The main reason transformers became so important is that they changed how models process language.

172
00:13:35,136 --> 00:13:41,092
Instead of reading text one word at a time, transformers allow every token to look at the other tokens at the same time.

173
00:13:41,422 --> 00:13:45,892
This helped models understand context better and made large-scale training much faster.

174
00:13:46,350 --> 00:13:51,012
This idea also made it possible to train much larger models like GPU efficiently.

175
00:13:51,566 --> 00:13:55,136
Before transformers, language models commonly used recurrent neural networks.

176
00:13:55,406 --> 00:14:00,256
Recurrent neural networks process text sequentially, one token at a time, from left to right.

177
00:14:00,494 --> 00:14:05,028
The main issue was the RNN depend on a hidden state to carry information from earlier tokens.

178
00:14:05,296 --> 00:14:10,756
As sentences became longer, important information from beginning could become weak, compressed, or forgotten.

179
00:14:11,054 --> 00:14:14,628
This made it difficult for RNN to understand long-range relationships.

180
00:14:14,896 --> 00:14:20,416
For example, in a long paragraph, a word at the end may depend on a word or idea mentioned much earlier.

181
00:14:20,656 --> 00:14:25,668
RNN were also difficult to train efficiently because they could not process all tokens at the same time.

182
00:14:25,998 --> 00:14:30,096
Each step depended on the previous step, which made training slow and limited scalability.

183
00:14:30,350 --> 00:14:32,164
So why transformers changed AI?

184
00:14:32,416 --> 00:14:35,172
Transformers solved the major problems of older models.

185
00:14:35,376 --> 00:14:39,684
They removed strict sequential processing and allowed all tokens to be processed in parallel.

186
00:14:39,950 --> 00:14:43,332
This means every token can attend to every other token at the same time.

187
00:14:43,566 --> 00:14:46,052
There is no hidden state bottleneck like RNN.

188
00:14:46,286 --> 00:14:50,576
So the model can capture relationships between words even when they are far apart in a sentence.

189
00:14:50,670 --> 00:14:54,756
Because transformers support parallel training and they can scale much better on GPUs.

190
00:14:55,182 --> 00:14:58,372
This is one of the main reasons modern LLMs became possible.

191
00:14:58,606 --> 00:15:04,260
As more data and computing power became available, transformer models could grow larger and more capable.

192
00:15:04,718 --> 00:15:07,556
So tokenization is the first step in the transformer pipeline.

193
00:15:07,790 --> 00:15:12,096
Since computers do not directly understand words, text must be converted into numerical form.

194
00:15:12,334 --> 00:15:14,256
A tokenizer breaks text into tokens.

195
00:15:14,416 --> 00:15:19,696
A token can be a full word, part of a word, punctuation mark, number, or space-like symbol.

196
00:15:19,856 --> 00:15:22,116
Each token is assigned a unique token ID.

197
00:15:22,336 --> 00:15:29,124
For example, a sentence is first split into tokens and then each token is converted into an integer that the model can process.

198
00:15:29,710 --> 00:15:38,500
Modern LLMs often use methods like bytepair encoding, where common words may stay as one token, while rare or complex words may be split into smaller pieces.

199
00:15:38,926 --> 00:15:45,412
Tokenization is important because token counts affects context length, memory usage, processing speed, and API cost.

200
00:15:46,030 --> 00:15:49,380
So after tokenization, token IDs are converted into embeddings.

201
00:15:49,616 --> 00:15:54,336
An embedding is a dense vector or a list of numbers that represent the meaning of a token.

202
00:15:54,574 --> 00:15:56,896
So token IDs alone do not contain meaning.

203
00:15:57,070 --> 00:16:00,452
For example, the number assigned to a token is just an index.

204
00:16:00,750 --> 00:16:03,748
Embeddings turn that index into a meaningful representation.

205
00:16:04,142 --> 00:16:09,828
So during training, the model learns these embeddings by seeing how words and tokens appear in the context.

206
00:16:10,542 --> 00:16:14,788
Tokens with similar meanings or usage patterns tend to have similar vector representations.

207
00:16:15,120 --> 00:16:21,412
For example, words related to food, technology, emotions, or locations may form meaningful patterns in embedding space.

208
00:16:21,646 --> 00:16:27,716
Embeddings help the transformer understand relationships between words, concepts, and context before attention is applied.

209
00:16:28,206 --> 00:16:30,960
So self-attention is the core mechanism of transformers.

210
00:16:31,120 --> 00:16:36,160
It allows each token to look at the other tokens in the same sentence and decide which ones are most important.

211
00:16:36,334 --> 00:16:38,948
This is useful because meaning often depends on the context.

212
00:16:39,246 --> 00:16:43,428
For example, in the sentence, the animal did not cross the road because it was tired.

213
00:16:43,680 --> 00:16:46,308
The model needs to understand what it refers to.

214
00:16:46,480 --> 00:16:49,840
Self-attention uses three vectors query, key, and value.

215
00:16:49,998 --> 00:16:52,400
The query represents what a token is looking for.

216
00:16:52,590 --> 00:16:57,316
The key represents what each token offers and the value contains the information passed forward.

217
00:16:57,742 --> 00:17:01,200
So the model comprises queries and keys to calculate attention scores.

218
00:17:01,390 --> 00:17:04,640
Higher scores mean one token should pay more attention to the other token.

219
00:17:04,960 --> 00:17:11,520
This allows transformers to capture relationships between nearby words and distant words, making them much better at understanding the context.

220
00:17:11,694 --> 00:17:14,372
So, what is multi-head retention and positional encoding?

221
00:17:14,606 --> 00:17:18,500
Multi-head retention means the model runs many attention operations at the same time.

222
00:17:18,798 --> 00:17:21,796
Each attention head can focus on a different type of relationship.

223
00:17:22,126 --> 00:17:28,004
One head may focus on the grammar, another on pronouns, other on meaning, and the other on long-range context.

224
00:17:28,430 --> 00:17:31,780
These outputs are combined to create a richer understanding of the sentence.

225
00:17:32,686 --> 00:17:36,708
Since transformer process tokens in parallel, they also need positional encoding.

226
00:17:37,038 --> 00:17:42,560
Positional encoding helps the model understand word order because order changes meaning in the language.

227
00:17:42,720 --> 00:17:47,972
For example, the dog chased the cat and the cat chased the dog contains the same words but different meanings.

228
00:17:48,526 --> 00:17:51,556
A transformer block combines multiple important components.

229
00:17:51,760 --> 00:17:54,788
It starts with input embeddings plus positional information.

230
00:17:55,182 --> 00:17:59,748
Then multi-head self-attention is applied so that the model can understand relationships between the tokens.

231
00:18:00,110 --> 00:18:06,212
After that, residual connections and layer normalization help training remain stable and prevent useful information from being lost.

232
00:18:07,054 --> 00:18:12,000
The block also includes a feedforward network which further processes each token's representation.

233
00:18:12,160 --> 00:18:16,480
Many transformer blocks are stacked together and each layer builds deeper language understanding.

234
00:18:16,640 --> 00:18:21,440
Lower layers may learn basic patterns while higher layers learn more complex meaning and reasoning.

235
00:18:21,742 --> 00:18:23,876
So now let's look into transformer architecture types.

236
00:18:24,160 --> 00:18:26,212
There are three main transformer architecture types.

237
00:18:26,510 --> 00:18:32,420
Encoder-only models are useful for classification, named entity, recognition, semantic search and embeddings.

238
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:35,200
Decoder only models are used for text generation.

239
00:18:35,406 --> 00:18:41,520
Models like GPT, Lama, Claude, and Mistral follow this style because they generate text one token at a time.

240
00:18:41,806 --> 00:18:48,292
Encoder-decoder models are useful for sequence-to-sequence tasks like translation, summarization, and question generation.

241
00:18:48,654 --> 00:18:52,260
The encoder understands the input and the decoder generates the output.

242
00:18:52,480 --> 00:18:58,800
Each transformer type is useful for different tasks depending on whether the goal is understanding generation or both.

243
